        <mxCell id="c36" value="&lt;b&gt;Good-sound band&lt;/b&gt; of speaker A (blue) and B (orange): ±30° around each speaker's axis (LP-6 V2 listening window, flat response). The LP-6 is specified for 120° coverage, so up to ±60° is still usable but gets duller. &lt;b&gt;Darker overlap = stereo sweet zone.&lt;/b&gt; Speakers are toed-in towards the centre of the group." style="text;html=1;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="215.0" y="940.0" width="565.0" height="60.0" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="c37" value="&lt;b&gt;v3 for comparison&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt; Smallest max distance (1.01 m) and average (0.75 m), all reach all controllers.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt; &lt;b&gt;Speakers:&lt;/b&gt; all four players are well inside both ±30° bands (max 12° off-axis), but the speakers are 2.4 m away and only 0.9 m apart, so the stereo angle is just 19° (ideal ≈ 60°). The stereo image will be narrow. Moving the speakers further apart is not possible on the Schrank (1.55 m long) without moving the group closer.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt; Compare with page 1 (your idea), which gives a 54° stereo angle at the cost of a 1.30 m max distance." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10;fillColor=#f9f9f9;strokeColor=#999999;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
